export abstract anchor FactoryRecovery:
    restartRecovery:
        The factory must recover orders after a crash or restart. It preserves
        plans, step status, briefs, results, and worktree references. Before
        resuming, it checks interrupted steps so completed work is not blindly
        repeated. If an agent is still working in its harness after a factory
        restart, reconnect to that session and let it continue rather than
        launching a replacement. Preserve the session references needed for
        this recovery so surviving work is not duplicated. Launch, discovery,
        and uncertain liveness follow FactorySessions.liveness.
    recoveryInventory:
        Startup acquires the writer lock and reconciles in this order: store and
        audit integrity; configuration digest and retained snapshots; plans and
        worktree ownership; binding of effect intents and idempotency claims;
        target delivery classification under the old epoch; sessions; attempts;
        jobs; deliveries and receipts; retry and breaker state; reservations,
        unresolved exposure, queue, and capacity.
        Claims are bound and quarantined before evidence, then disposed after
        the session, target, or job evidence that determines their outcome. No
        launch, build, delivery, worktree, or retry is admitted until all stages
        complete and the claim is disposed. Scopes are global, account, route,
        target, order, and plan subtree; an order-local failure pauses only that
        order.
    unresolvedRecovery:
        Unresolved attempts keep their effect key. Positive terminal session
        evidence or a confirmed stop transitions unresolved to interrupted. An
        engineer confirming no effect transitions it to failed. An authorized
        replacement transitions it to orphaned and creates a new attempt; the
        old attempt stays terminal and its lease and effect key are transferred
        explicitly.
        A live session found after restart while unresolved is not resumed
        automatically. The engineer chooses stop, replacement, or acceptance of
        the result after re-based assessment. In-flight deliveries are
        classified under the old epoch before re-fencing; applied adopts or
        reconstructs the receipt under that epoch, not-applied releases, and
        unknown holds the claim. Only free or expired leases are re-fenced. A
        late holder is recorded as orphaned, and non-git adapters report whether
        the effect landed.
    retryRecovery:
        Retry state is an explicit record per attempt chain with a stable timer
        identifier, effect key, chain generation, policy generation, counted
        attempts, remaining allowance, wall-clock absolute deadline, delay
        source, and non-counting reasons. Breaker state records account,
        harness, route, the failure-event window, cooldown, a single half-open
        probe lease, and its policy generation.
        Fire-once consumption, retry-record advance, attempt intent, idempotency
        claim, and admission reservation commit atomically. The fire-once key
        includes effect key, chain generation, and retry ordinal. Restart
        resumes the wall-clock deadline; downtime past it fires once during
        reconciliation through the full admission gate. Breaker reconciliation
        is account and route scoped, preserves the window, and permits exactly
        one probe.
    recoveryMatrix:
        Recovery conformance uses crash points derived from the durability
        order: before claim, after claim before intent, after intent before
        launch acknowledgement, after effect before outcome commit, after
        read-back before receipt commit, during adoption, during compensation,
        after outcome commit before acknowledgement, and during reconciliation
        itself. Cells cover live-session restart, unresolved restart,
        in-doubt delivery, expired lease with a stale holder attempting an
        effect, restart during backoff, duplicate timer fire, and duplicate
        request after restart.
        Each cell states the expected persisted state, launch and adapter
        invocation counts cross-checked against intents and receipts, expected
        configuration digest, and a path-reached assertion. A negative control
        disables fencing and must detect a duplicate and fail. Forcing lookup to
        unknown is not a failure control because unknown must hold the claim and
        produce no further effect. Quiescence advances past persisted deadlines.
        A cell that never reaches its fault is not passed.
    unlikelyResolution:
        When further autonomous attempts no longer look likely to resolve a
        problem, obtain engineer input rather than continuing unsuccessful
        repair or review cycles. Apply this through the existing escalation
        path and continue independent work where possible. This decision does
        not establish a fixed attempt count or time limit for substantive
        problem resolution.
    gracefulShutdown:
        On graceful shutdown, the factory stops starting new steps, waits a
        configurable grace period for running steps to finish, then preserves
        unfinished steps and pending questions as resumable and exits. The
        report distinguishes finished, interrupted, still-running, and
        awaiting-input work. The engineer may choose to wait longer when
        prompted. Shutdown never discards preserved work.
